::ENG3_U03_L06_GP01::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: People of the city, look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children waiting at the market. If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. What rhetorical choice appears in "look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children"? {
~A. A definition of grain prices.#No definition is given. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Repetition.#Correct. The phrase "look at" is repeated to focus attention. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. A list of laws.#The passage lists images, not laws. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. A personal anecdote.#No personal story is told. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U03_L06_GP02::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: People of the city, look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children waiting at the market. If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. What effect does the repetition create? {
~A. It makes the problem seem distant and unimportant.#The repeated images make the problem immediate. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. It hides the speaker's purpose.#It makes the purpose clearer. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. It builds urgency by forcing the audience to picture several signs of need.#Correct. The repetition creates pressure and urgency. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. It proves that the city has enough food.#The images show shortage. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U03_L06_GP03::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. What rhetorical choice is used in this sentence? {
