}

::ENG3_U03_L06_GP04::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: People of the city, look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children waiting at the market. If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. What is the author's likely purpose? {
=A. To persuade the city to act quickly on the grain shipment.#Correct. The images and warning push for immediate action. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. To entertain citizens with a story about a market.#The passage is persuasive, not mainly entertaining. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. To explain how mills are built.#The mill is an image, not a construction topic. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. To argue against feeding children.#The passage presents children's need as urgent. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U03_L06_GP05::[MLA.ENG3.R.12] Passage: People of the city, look at the empty granary, look at the silent mill, look at the children waiting at the market. If we delay the grain shipment, hunger will speak louder than any council bell. Which evaluation is strongest? {
